The leaky bucket algorithm manages traffic flow by simulating a bucket with a small hole at the bottom. **Token bucket** holds tokens generated at a fixed rate, one every T seconds. A packet can be sent only by taking a token. Idle hosts save tokens (up to the bucket size), so short bursts can be sent quickly. [p. 2] In contrast, **leaky bucket** does not let bursts through, even if the host was idle before. If the bucket is full, extra tokens spill out, discarding tokens instead of packets. [p. 2] This ensures smooth, fixed-rate flow even during bursts. [p. 2] A simple example of leaky bucket implementation could be:

```python
class TokenBucket:
    def __init__(self, rate, bucket_size):
        self.tokens = bucket_size
        self.rate = rate

    def send_packet(self, packet_size):
        if self.tokens >= packet_size:
            self.tokens -= packet_size
            return True
        else:
            return False
```

This code snippet shows how tokens are consumed for each packet sent, with the bucket size and rate parameters controlling the flow. [p. 2]
